From: Project Arctic
Subject: mscoree: with the .NET Framework installed, it is the Framework's shim, as in Windows 8 and later.

Since Windows 8 mscoree.dll belongs to the system: a shim whose exports go
on to mscoreei.dll of the installed .NET Framework, which picks and starts
the runtime. The Framework's installer for these systems brings mscoreei,
clr and the rest, but no mscoree, so a .NET program in Arctic found Wine's,
which runs only Mono, which Arctic has not got: the G HUB installer (WPF,
.NET 4.7.2) ended at once after User Account Control, with no window.

Now, when InstallRoot\v4.0.30319\mscoreei.dll is there, every export of
mscoree jumps to mscoreei's (the arguments as the caller left them, so one
jump fits every signature), and DllMain tells mscoreei of each call
(OnShimDllMainCalled). The ordinals are mscoreei's: the runtime asks
mscoree for 24 by its number, with no name, and mscoreei asks it, from its
own DllMain, for 142, the version of the system as it is (an
OSVERSIONINFOW). Without the Framework every export is Wine's own, as
before. Verified: RegAsm.exe and the G HUB installer on .NET 4.8.
